<p>Artificial intelligence is moving from research labs into everyday clinics and hospitals. Machine learning models now help doctors spot patterns in medical scans, predict patient risks, and speed up drug discovery.<br>
 <h4>Faster Diagnostics:</h4> AI tools analyze X-rays, MRIs, and pathology slides quickly. They flag subtle anomalies that busy clinicians might miss.
<h4> Personalized Care:</h4> Algorithms combine genetics, lifestyle data, and treatment history to recommend targeted therapies.
 <h4>Administrative Relief:</h4> Voice-to-text assistants draft clinical notes and schedule follow-ups, giving doctors more time with patients.<br>
Challenges remain before AI can operate unchecked in medicine. Biased training data can skew results for underrepresented groups, and hospital systems must protect sensitive patient privacy. Clear clinical validation and human oversight keep technology in a supporting role.<br></p>
