<h1>Edge Computing: Bringing Intelligence Closer to Smart Cities</h1>
<p>Modern cities rely on thousands of connected sensors, cameras, and meters. Sending every byte of data to a distant cloud server creates delays and bandwidth bottlenecks. Edge computing solves this by processing data right where it is generated.
<h4>Faster traffic and safety response:</h4> Smart intersections and transit sensors adjust signals in real time to ease congestion and respond quickly to hazards.
 <h4>Smarter energy grids:</h4> Local nodes balance power loads across neighborhoods and integrate renewable sources more efficiently.
 <h4>Lower bandwidth costs:</h4> Filtering routine data locally means only critical alerts and summaries travel over city networks.<br>
Security and maintenance are key priorities as edge networks grow. Municipalities must keep local hardware patched against cyber threats and ensure reliable connectivity across dense urban areas. By cutting latency, edge computing helps turn raw urban data into timely action.
