case 'probability_vs_non_probablity':%>
<article class ="content" style="background-color: #F0FDFF; margin: 50px 50px; border-radius: 20px; border: 5px solid #3498db; padding:20px 20px 20px 20px;">
<h1>Probability vs. Non-Probability Sample Design</h1>
<p>Choosing the right sample design shapes whether your research findings can generalize to a wider group. Researchers generally divide sample designs into two categories: probability and non-probability.
<h4> Probability sampling:</h4> Every unit in the target population has a known, non-zero chance of selection. Common methods include simple random, systematic, stratified, and cluster sampling. Because selection is random, researchers can measure sampling error and estimate population parameters statistically.
<h4> Non-probability sampling:</h4> Selection relies on judgment, convenience, or referrals rather than random chance. Methods include convenience, purposive, quota, and snowball sampling. These designs cost less and run faster, making them useful for exploratory or qualitative studies, though they carry higher bias risk.<br>
Strong research matches the method to the study goal, budget, and required accuracy. For a deeper look at definitions and concepts, see Unit- Two Sampling Design.<br></p>
</article>
