case 'core_steps_in_design':%>
<article class ="content" style="background-color: #F0FDFF; margin: 50px 50px; border-radius: 20px; border: 5px solid #3498db; padding:20px 20px 20px 20px;">
<h1>Five Core Steps in Building a Sample Design</h1>
<p>A clear sample design prevents wasted budget and skewed data before fieldwork begins. Most studies follow five practical steps:<br>
    <h4>Define the target population:</h4> Specify the exact group, location, and timeframe the study covers.
 <h4>Build the sampling frame:</h4> Gather an accurate list or map of eligible units, removing duplicates or missing entries.
<h4>Select the sampling method:</h4> Decide between probability and non-probability techniques based on precision needs and resources.
 <h4>Determine the sample size:</h4> Balance desired margin of error and population variability against time and cost limits.
 <h4>Execute and monitor:</h4> Draw the sample consistently and track non-response or coverage issues during collection.<br></p>
</article>
