case 'legal_challenges_of _india':%>
<article class ="content" style="background-color: #F0FDFF; margin: 50px 50px; border-radius: 20px; border: 5px solid #3498db; padding:20px 20px 20px 20px;">
<h1> Data Privacy and AI-Generated Deepfakes: Legal Challenges in India</h1>
<p>Introduction<br>
AI-generated deepfakes create realistic audio, images, and videos that can impersonate real people without consent. In India, the rapid spread of non-consensual and deceptive deepfakes raises urgent questions around privacy, reputation, and digital accountability.<br>
<h3>Existing Legal Framework</h3>
<h4>Constitutional Privacy:</h4> Under Article 21 of the Constitution of India, the right to privacy is a fundamental right, protecting individual autonomy, dignity, and personal data.
<h4>Information Technology Rules:</h4> Intermediaries such as social media platforms face due-diligence obligations under the Information Technology (Intermediary Guidelines and Digital Media Ethics Code) Rules to remove unlawful or harmful content promptly upon notice.
<h4>Data Protection:</h4> India’s Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 establishes rules for processing personal data and requires lawful grounds and safeguards, though enforcement timelines and practical overlap with emerging AI risks remain closely watched.
<h3>Key Challenges and Way Forward</h3>
Identifying the original creator of a deepfake is often difficult when files are shared across encrypted or anonymous channels. Courts and lawmakers must balance swift takedown mechanisms and clear liability rules with constitutional protections for free speech and lawful creative expression.<br></p>
</article>
