<article class ="content" style="background-color: #F0FDFF; margin: 50px 50px; border-radius: 20px; border: 5px solid #3498db; padding:20px 20px 20px 20px;">
<h1>Why Static Shocks Happen More Often in Winter</h1>
<p>Dry winter air makes static shocks more common. Cold air holds less water vapor than warm air. When cold air warms up indoors, its relative humidity drops.
Water molecules in the air help carry away extra electrical charges from surfaces. In dry air, charges build up easily when you walk across a carpet or pull off a sweater. Touching metal gives those trapped electrons a quick path to jump, creating a small spark and shock. Using a humidifier or wearing natural fibers can reduce charge buildup.</p>
</article>
