<h1>How Greenhouse Gases Warm the Planet</h1>
<p>Human activities have raised global temperatures since the mid-20th century by strengthening the greenhouse effect. Sunlight warms Earth's surface, which then sends heat back toward space as infrared radiation.
Gases like carbon dioxide (CO₂) absorb some of this outgoing heat and re-emit it in all directions, including back toward the surface. Burning fossil fuels such as coal and oil releases extra CO₂ into the atmosphere. Higher CO₂ levels trap more heat and drive long-term climate change.</p>
</article>
